WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.336 --> 00:11.156 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference December 18,

00:11.716 --> 00:13.706 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

00:14.076 --> 00:14.706 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:14.976 --> 00:17.306 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:17.306 --> 00:20.476 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:20.606 --> 00:23.326 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:24.576 --> 00:26.066 align:middle
The economy is strong overall

00:26.066 --> 00:28.196 align:middle
and has made significant
progress toward our,

00:28.196 --> 00:30.446 align:middle
our goals over the
past two years.

00:31.346 --> 00:32.506 align:middle
The labor market has cooled

00:32.506 --> 00:35.546 align:middle
from its formerly overheated
state and remains solid.

00:36.576 --> 00:38.326 align:middle
Inflation has moved much closer

00:38.326 --> 00:40.316 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

00:42.226 --> 00:44.466 align:middle
We're committed to maintaining
our economy's strength

00:44.466 --> 00:47.346 align:middle
by supporting maximum employment
and returning inflation

00:47.346 --> 00:48.456 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal.

00:49.446 --> 00:52.226 align:middle
To that end, today, the Federal
Open Market Committee decided

00:52.226 --> 00:55.236 align:middle
to take another step in reducing
the degree of policy restraint

00:55.966 --> 00:58.966 align:middle
by lowering our policy interest
rate by ¼ percentage point.

01:00.646 --> 01:03.606 align:middle
We also decided to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

01:04.396 --> 01:06.576 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

01:06.576 --> 01:08.856 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:11.316 --> 01:14.226 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

01:14.226 --> 01:16.586 align:middle
to expand at a-at a solid pace.

01:17.636 --> 01:20.616 align:middle
GDP rose at an annual
rate of 2.8 percent

01:20.696 --> 01:23.066 align:middle
in the third quarter,
about the same pace

01:23.066 --> 01:24.036 align:middle
as in the second quarter.

01:25.206 --> 01:27.526 align:middle
Growth of consumer spending
has remained resilient,

01:28.216 --> 01:31.456 align:middle
and investment in equipment and
intangibles has strengthened.

01:32.416 --> 01:35.706 align:middle
In contrast, activity in the
housing sector has been weak.

01:37.176 --> 01:39.726 align:middle
Overall, improving supply
conditions have supported the

01:39.726 --> 01:42.496 align:middle
strong performance of the U.S.
economy over the past year.

01:43.766 --> 01:45.586 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

01:45.716 --> 01:48.636 align:middle
Committee participants
generally expect GDP growth

01:48.636 --> 01:52.226 align:middle
to remain solid, with a median
projection of about 2 percent

01:52.456 --> 01:53.806 align:middle
over the next few years.

01:55.516 --> 01:57.586 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions remain solid.

01:58.226 --> 02:01.196 align:middle
Payroll job gains have slowed
from earlier in the year,

02:01.636 --> 02:05.256 align:middle
averaging 173,000 per month
over the past three months.

02:06.656 --> 02:09.956 align:middle
The unemployment rate is higher
than it was a year ago, but,

02:09.956 --> 02:13.076 align:middle
at 4.2 percent in November,
it has remained low.

02:14.436 --> 02:17.016 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year,

02:17.216 --> 02:19.376 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed.

02:20.876 --> 02:23.466 align:middle
Overall, a broad set
of indicators suggests

02:23.506 --> 02:25.966 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are now less tight

02:26.406 --> 02:28.076 align:middle
than in 2019.

02:29.706 --> 02:31.456 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

02:31.456 --> 02:33.396 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

02:33.876 --> 02:37.296 align:middle
The median projection
for the unemployment rate

02:37.296 --> 02:42.236 align:middle
in the SEP is 4.2 percent at the
end of this year and 4.3 percent

02:42.416 --> 02:43.706 align:middle
over the next few years.

02:45.976 --> 02:47.936 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly

02:47.936 --> 02:49.196 align:middle
over the past two years

02:49.286 --> 02:51.336 align:middle
but remains somewhat
elevated relative

02:51.386 --> 02:53.176 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:54.496 --> 02:56.756 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

02:56.756 --> 02:58.246 align:middle
and other data indicate

02:58.246 --> 03:01.846 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.5 percent

03:02.476 --> 03:05.266 align:middle
over the 12 months ending
in November, and that,

03:05.626 --> 03:07.976 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:08.436 --> 03:11.046 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.8 percent.

03:12.466 --> 03:14.526 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

03:14.526 --> 03:17.406 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

03:17.406 --> 03:20.656 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

03:21.206 --> 03:23.186 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

03:24.656 --> 03:26.326 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

03:26.326 --> 03:29.736 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
is 2.4 percent this year

03:30.166 --> 03:33.226 align:middle
and 2.5 percent next
year, somewhat higher

03:33.226 --> 03:34.566 align:middle
than projected in September.

03:36.476 --> 03:38.306 align:middle
Thereafter, the median
projection falls

03:38.306 --> 03:39.636 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective.

03:41.376 --> 03:44.766 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:44.806 --> 03:48.006 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:48.006 --> 03:49.006 align:middle
for the American people.

03:50.516 --> 03:52.416 align:middle
We see the risks to
achieving our employment

03:52.416 --> 03:54.926 align:middle
and inflation goals as
being roughly in balance,

03:55.666 --> 03:57.146 align:middle
and we are attentive
to the risks

03:57.196 --> 03:58.596 align:middle
on both sides of our mandate.

04:00.146 --> 04:01.926 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

04:01.926 --> 04:04.096 align:middle
to lower the target range
for the federal funds rate

04:04.126 --> 04:07.576 align:middle
by ¼ percentage point,
to 4¼-4½ percent.

04:08.506 --> 04:11.426 align:middle
We've been moving policy toward
a more neutral setting in order

04:11.426 --> 04:13.216 align:middle
to maintain the strength
of the economy

04:13.216 --> 04:16.366 align:middle
and the labor market while
establishing further progress

04:16.446 --> 04:18.806 align:middle
in-sorry, enabling further
progress on-inflation.

04:20.246 --> 04:22.886 align:middle
With today's action, we
have lowered our policy rate

04:22.886 --> 04:24.816 align:middle
by a full percentage
point from its peak,

04:25.686 --> 04:28.946 align:middle
and our policy stance is now
significantly less restrictive.

04:30.766 --> 04:32.386 align:middle
We can therefore
be more cautious

04:32.496 --> 04:35.146 align:middle
as we consider further
adjustments to our policy rate.

04:36.796 --> 04:39.476 align:middle
We know that reducing
policy restraint too fast

04:39.476 --> 04:42.326 align:middle
or too much could hinder
progress on inflation.

04:43.326 --> 04:46.266 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too slowly

04:46.326 --> 04:49.706 align:middle
or too little could unduly
weaken economic activity

04:49.836 --> 04:50.496 align:middle
and employment.

04:51.886 --> 04:54.606 align:middle
In considering the extent and
timing of additional adjustments

04:54.606 --> 04:56.436 align:middle
to the target range for
the federal funds rate,

04:57.256 --> 04:58.956 align:middle
the Committee will
assess incoming data,

04:59.196 --> 05:01.366 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

05:02.336 --> 05:04.016 align:middle
We're not on any preset course.

05:05.746 --> 05:07.566 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

05:07.816 --> 05:10.946 align:middle
FOMC participants wrote down
their individual assessments

05:10.976 --> 05:13.156 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

05:13.626 --> 05:15.556 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

05:15.906 --> 05:18.286 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario going forward.

05:18.796 --> 05:23.166 align:middle
The median participant projects
that the appropriate level

05:23.166 --> 05:26.506 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 3.9 percent at the end

05:26.506 --> 05:30.936 align:middle
of next year and 3.4
percent at the end of 2026.

05:31.756 --> 05:33.866 align:middle
These median projections
are somewhat higher

05:33.866 --> 05:35.556 align:middle
than in September, consistent

05:35.556 --> 05:37.176 align:middle
with the firmer inflation
projection.

05:38.226 --> 05:39.316 align:middle
These projections, however,

05:39.566 --> 05:41.316 align:middle
are not a Committee
plan or decision.

05:42.786 --> 05:47.186 align:middle
As the economy evolves, monetary
policy will adjust in order

05:47.186 --> 05:49.116 align:middle
to best promote our
maximum-employment

05:49.116 --> 05:50.426 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

05:51.446 --> 05:54.286 align:middle
If the economy remains strong
and inflation does not continue

05:54.286 --> 05:56.396 align:middle
to move sustainably
toward 2 percent,

05:56.916 --> 05:59.246 align:middle
we can dial back policy
restraint more slowly.

06:00.626 --> 06:02.646 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

06:03.026 --> 06:05.566 align:middle
or inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

06:06.236 --> 06:07.716 align:middle
we can ease policy more quickly.

06:08.736 --> 06:11.376 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

06:11.426 --> 06:12.926 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

06:13.416 --> 06:15.986 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

06:17.636 --> 06:19.836 align:middle
On a technical note: We
lowered the offering rate

06:19.836 --> 06:22.906 align:middle
on our overnight reverse
repo facility to align it

06:22.906 --> 06:24.276 align:middle
with the bottom of
the target range

06:24.276 --> 06:27.146 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate-its typical configuration.

06:28.196 --> 06:30.336 align:middle
Technical adjustments of
this kind have no bearing

06:30.406 --> 06:31.996 align:middle
on the stance of
monetary policy.

06:33.626 --> 06:36.666 align:middle
The Fed has been assigned two
goals for monetary policy:

06:36.836 --> 06:38.846 align:middle
maximum employment
and stable prices.

06:39.586 --> 06:42.036 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

06:42.616 --> 06:45.446 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

06:45.916 --> 06:48.506 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

06:48.506 --> 06:49.106 align:middle
well anchored.

06:50.036 --> 06:51.206 align:middle
Our success in delivering

06:51.206 --> 06:53.666 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

06:54.446 --> 06:57.246 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

06:57.246 --> 06:58.886 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

07:00.046 --> 07:02.406 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:03.326 --> 07:05.016 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:05.016 --> 07:06.666 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:07.006 --> 07:08.206 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:08.836 --> 07:11.046 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

07:11.646 --> 07:12.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:12.386 --> 07:15.166 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

07:16.806 --> 07:18.146 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

07:18.376 --> 07:19.746 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

07:20.146 --> 07:22.536 align:middle
I, I wonder if you
could talk a little bit

07:22.756 --> 07:27.196 align:middle
about why officials think it's
appropriate to cut rates at all

07:27.196 --> 07:29.586 align:middle
in 2025 if inflation is expected

07:29.586 --> 07:31.346 align:middle
to remain firm throughout
the year.

07:31.916 --> 07:34.416 align:middle
And what would you
expect, at this point,

07:34.766 --> 07:36.056 align:middle
the timing might look like?

07:36.056 --> 07:38.636 align:middle
Would a January cut
potentially be possible,

07:38.636 --> 07:40.786 align:middle
or does a pause next
month seem more likely?

07:41.976 --> 07:42.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:42.446 --> 07:45.846 align:middle
Well, so let, let me start by
saying why we-why we cut today

07:45.846 --> 07:47.576 align:middle
and then-and then move to 2025.

07:47.576 --> 07:50.166 align:middle
So I would say today
was a-was a closer call,

07:50.726 --> 07:52.916 align:middle
but we decided it
was the right call

07:52.996 --> 07:56.106 align:middle
because we thought it
was the best decision

07:56.106 --> 07:59.096 align:middle
to foster achievement
of both of our goals,

07:59.096 --> 08:00.606 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

08:01.086 --> 08:03.996 align:middle
We see the risks as
two-sided: moving too slowly

08:03.996 --> 08:06.716 align:middle
and needlessly undermine
economic activity

08:06.716 --> 08:08.406 align:middle
and the labor market,
or move too quickly

08:08.846 --> 08:11.046 align:middle
and needlessly undermine
our progress on inflation.

08:11.476 --> 08:14.376 align:middle
So we're trying to steer
between those two risks, and,

08:14.376 --> 08:16.436 align:middle
on balance, we decided to
go ahead with a further cut.

08:16.436 --> 08:18.246 align:middle
And I, I'll give you
some details on why.

08:19.266 --> 08:21.206 align:middle
Downside risks to the
labor market do appear

08:21.206 --> 08:23.786 align:middle
to have diminished, but the
labor market is now looser

08:23.786 --> 08:26.546 align:middle
than pre-pandemic, and it's
clearly still cooling further,

08:27.256 --> 08:29.866 align:middle
so far in, in a gradual
and orderly way.

08:30.646 --> 08:33.376 align:middle
We don't think we need further
cooling in the labor market

08:33.376 --> 08:35.006 align:middle
to get inflation
down to 2 percent.

08:35.746 --> 08:38.266 align:middle
Job creation is now
well below the level

08:38.266 --> 08:39.596 align:middle
or certainly below the level

08:39.596 --> 08:41.176 align:middle
that would hold unemployment
constant,

08:41.686 --> 08:44.376 align:middle
the job-finding rate
is low and declining,

08:44.946 --> 08:46.956 align:middle
and other measures-such
as surveys of workers

08:46.956 --> 08:50.736 align:middle
and businesses, quits, things
like that-broadly show a,

08:50.906 --> 08:55.226 align:middle
a much cooler labor market than
there was, than we had in 2019.

08:55.226 --> 08:57.036 align:middle
It's still quite
gradually cooling.

08:57.996 --> 08:59.726 align:middle
So we, we keep an eye on that.

09:00.396 --> 09:03.756 align:middle
Inflation, we see that story
as still broadly on track, and,

09:03.816 --> 09:04.766 align:middle
and I'll tell you why.

09:04.886 --> 09:06.356 align:middle
We, we've made a great
deal of progress.

09:06.356 --> 09:08.356 align:middle
Twelve-month core
inflation, as I mentioned,

09:08.826 --> 09:11.496 align:middle
through November is
estimated at 2.8 percent,

09:11.556 --> 09:13.356 align:middle
down from a high of 5.6 percent.

09:14.156 --> 09:16.676 align:middle
Twelve-but 12-month inflation
has actually been moving

09:16.676 --> 09:19.426 align:middle
sideways, as we are
"lapping" very low readings

09:19.566 --> 09:20.676 align:middle
of late last year.

09:21.396 --> 09:24.046 align:middle
Housing services inflation
actually is steadily coming

09:24.046 --> 09:26.716 align:middle
down now, albeit at a slower
pace than we might like,

09:26.766 --> 09:28.516 align:middle
but it has now come
down substantially

09:28.516 --> 09:32.656 align:middle
and it is making
progress, slower than hoped.

09:33.116 --> 09:36.836 align:middle
And we, we've had recent high
readings from nonmarket services

09:36.836 --> 09:37.886 align:middle
and some bumpiness for goods.

09:37.886 --> 09:41.316 align:middle
So I'll just say,
we, we-so remember

09:41.316 --> 09:43.896 align:middle
that we couple this decision
today with the "extent

09:43.896 --> 09:46.046 align:middle
and timing" language in
the postmeeting statement

09:46.366 --> 09:48.646 align:middle
that signals that we
are at or near a point

09:48.646 --> 09:50.446 align:middle
at which it will be
appropriate to slow the pace

09:50.446 --> 09:51.506 align:middle
of further adjustments.

09:52.236 --> 09:53.856 align:middle
You ask about 2025.

09:54.556 --> 09:59.396 align:middle
I, I think that the
lower-the slower pace of cuts

09:59.396 --> 10:04.426 align:middle
for next year really reflects
both the higher inflation

10:04.426 --> 10:07.076 align:middle
readings we've had this
year and the expectation

10:07.076 --> 10:08.276 align:middle
that inflation will be higher.

10:08.726 --> 10:12.146 align:middle
You saw in the SEP that
risks and, and uncertainty

10:12.186 --> 10:14.446 align:middle
around inflation
we see as higher.

10:14.876 --> 10:17.306 align:middle
Nonetheless, we, we
see ourselves as,

10:17.556 --> 10:20.196 align:middle
as still on track
to continue to cut.

10:20.196 --> 10:23.046 align:middle
I think the actual cuts that
we make next year will not be

10:23.576 --> 10:25.286 align:middle
because of anything
we wrote down today.

10:25.286 --> 10:26.776 align:middle
We're, we're going
to react to data.

10:26.776 --> 10:27.986 align:middle
That's just the general sense

10:27.986 --> 10:30.106 align:middle
of what the Committee thinks
is likely to be appropriate.

10:30.656 --> 10:31.486 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

10:31.596 --> 10:34.626 align:middle
Sorry, just one, one quick
follow-up: Why, I guess,

10:34.626 --> 10:35.476 align:middle
would you make those cuts?

10:35.966 --> 10:37.686 align:middle
Like, what would-what would
be the trigger to cut?

10:38.006 --> 10:38.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:38.476 --> 10:41.496 align:middle
So we're-to, to cut
further after this point, I,

10:41.816 --> 10:43.526 align:middle
I would say it this way:

10:43.526 --> 10:46.556 align:middle
We've reduced our policy
rate now by 100 basis points.

10:46.656 --> 10:48.386 align:middle
We're significantly
closer to neutral.

10:49.036 --> 10:50.966 align:middle
At 4.3 percent and "change,"

10:50.966 --> 10:53.216 align:middle
we believe policy is still
meaningfully restrictive.

10:53.756 --> 10:55.916 align:middle
But as for additional cuts,
we're going to be looking

10:55.916 --> 10:58.596 align:middle
for further progress
on inflation as well

10:58.596 --> 11:00.126 align:middle
as continued strength
in the labor market.

11:00.166 --> 11:02.966 align:middle
And as long as the economy and
the labor market are solid,

11:03.066 --> 11:06.846 align:middle
we can be cautious about-as
we consider further cuts.

11:07.176 --> 11:10.676 align:middle
And all of that is reflected-to
your question-in the December

11:10.676 --> 11:12.466 align:middle
SEP, which shows
a median forecast

11:12.466 --> 11:15.196 align:middle
of down [to] two cuts next year,
compared to four in September.

11:16.226 --> 11:17.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:17.576 --> 11:18.916 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

11:19.566 --> 11:22.696 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters,
and thanks for the opportunity

11:22.696 --> 11:23.706 align:middle
to ask you a question.

11:23.986 --> 11:26.076 align:middle
So this does seem
similar to the dynamic

11:26.076 --> 11:28.686 align:middle
in 2016 during the
last transition

11:28.686 --> 11:30.026 align:middle
to a Trump Administration,

11:30.586 --> 11:34.096 align:middle
where the Committee saw
slightly tighter policy in part

11:35.006 --> 11:37.986 align:middle
in expected anticipation
of the fiscal policy stance

11:38.136 --> 11:40.286 align:middle
that was seen evolving
over the year.

11:40.746 --> 11:43.316 align:middle
Some of it was a data
mark-to-market exercise,

11:43.316 --> 11:45.226 align:middle
and some of it was
anticipation of fiscal.

11:45.846 --> 11:46.946 align:middle
What's the split on this one?

11:46.946 --> 11:49.526 align:middle
How much of this was
accounting for inflation data

11:49.836 --> 11:52.626 align:middle
that was coming in, and
how much of it is expecting

11:52.626 --> 11:55.206 align:middle
that there will be inflationary
fiscal policy next year?

11:55.956 --> 11:56.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:56.396 --> 11:59.216 align:middle
I, I'd say-I'd, I'd point
to five or six things, and,

11:59.306 --> 12:01.046 align:middle
and I'd start-let
me start by saying

12:01.046 --> 12:03.526 align:middle
that we think the economy's
in a really good place,

12:03.596 --> 12:05.266 align:middle
and we think policy's
in a really good place.

12:05.266 --> 12:08.486 align:middle
Let's remember that the
economy's growing 2½ percent

12:08.516 --> 12:12.116 align:middle
this year, that inflation has
come down by 50 percent to,

12:12.176 --> 12:14.946 align:middle
to-from 5.6 percent
to 2.6 percent.

12:15.286 --> 12:18.386 align:middle
Headline inflation is 2.5
percent on a 12-month basis.

12:18.386 --> 12:21.016 align:middle
We're actually in a really
good starting place here.

12:21.446 --> 12:24.706 align:middle
But since, you know-so, so
what's really driving the,

12:25.226 --> 12:27.156 align:middle
the slower rate-cut path?

12:27.156 --> 12:29.106 align:middle
First thing is growth
is stronger, right?

12:29.256 --> 12:35.386 align:middle
We, the economy grew faster in
the second half of 2024, so far,

12:35.476 --> 12:38.426 align:middle
than we had expected
and is expected to be

12:38.426 --> 12:41.536 align:middle
above what our expectations in
September next year as well.

12:42.056 --> 12:45.346 align:middle
Unemployment is lower,
and, you know, in the SEP,

12:45.346 --> 12:47.606 align:middle
you'll see that participants
think

12:47.646 --> 12:50.126 align:middle
that the-that the
downside risks are less

12:50.126 --> 12:51.266 align:middle
and uncertainty is less.

12:51.516 --> 12:54.526 align:middle
And so that's, that's
more strength, right?

12:55.216 --> 12:57.656 align:middle
Inflation is higher,
as we talked about.

12:57.656 --> 12:58.846 align:middle
Inflation's higher this year.

12:58.846 --> 13:00.606 align:middle
It's also higher in
the forecast next year.

13:01.276 --> 13:03.116 align:middle
I'd also point out that
we're, we're closer

13:03.116 --> 13:05.106 align:middle
to the neutral rate,
which is another reason

13:05.106 --> 13:07.156 align:middle
to be cautious about,
about further moves.

13:07.786 --> 13:11.376 align:middle
And-but then getting to your-to
your point, there's also,

13:11.636 --> 13:13.676 align:middle
there's uncertainty-uncertainty
around inflation,

13:13.676 --> 13:16.366 align:middle
I pointed out, is
actually higher.

13:16.866 --> 13:21.226 align:middle
It's also-it's also, in
the case of some people,

13:21.476 --> 13:23.056 align:middle
some people-the way
I'd say it is this:

13:23.466 --> 13:28.806 align:middle
Some people did take a very
preliminary step and start

13:28.806 --> 13:32.706 align:middle
to incorporate highly
conditional estimates

13:32.706 --> 13:35.366 align:middle
of economic effects of
policies into their forecast

13:35.366 --> 13:37.696 align:middle
at this meeting and
said so in the meeting.

13:37.696 --> 13:41.476 align:middle
Some people said they didn't
do so, and some people didn't,

13:41.476 --> 13:42.706 align:middle
didn't say whether
they did or not.

13:42.766 --> 13:45.956 align:middle
So we have people making a bunch
of different approaches to that.

13:46.556 --> 13:51.336 align:middle
But some did identify
policy uncertainty as one

13:51.336 --> 13:54.396 align:middle
of the reasons for
their, their writing

13:54.396 --> 13:56.276 align:middle
down more uncertainty
around inflation.

13:56.926 --> 13:59.606 align:middle
And, you know, the point
of that uncertainty is,

13:59.606 --> 14:01.276 align:middle
it's kind of commonsense
thinking

14:01.276 --> 14:03.816 align:middle
that when the path is uncertain
you go a little bit slower.

14:04.306 --> 14:07.126 align:middle
It's not unlike driving on
a foggy night or walking

14:07.126 --> 14:08.746 align:middle
into a dark room
full of furniture.

14:09.146 --> 14:10.046 align:middle
You just slow down.

14:10.046 --> 14:12.906 align:middle
And so that may have
affected some of the people.

14:12.906 --> 14:15.046 align:middle
But as, as I said,
there's a range of-range

14:15.046 --> 14:16.306 align:middle
of approaches on the Committee.

14:16.306 --> 14:16.856 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

14:16.856 --> 14:18.686 align:middle
If I could follow up on
that: You mentioned the risk

14:18.686 --> 14:21.496 align:middle
and uncertainty indexes toward
the back of the document.

14:21.916 --> 14:25.786 align:middle
The upside risk to inflation
jumped quite substantially.

14:26.346 --> 14:28.326 align:middle
The only thing, really, that's
happened-you, you mentioned

14:28.326 --> 14:30.786 align:middle
that the disinflationary
story remains intact,

14:31.466 --> 14:33.566 align:middle
yet the risk weighting
has jumped to the upside.

14:33.616 --> 14:36.246 align:middle
The only real thing that's
happened is November 5th,

14:36.246 --> 14:36.906 align:middle
in the meantime.

14:36.906 --> 14:39.846 align:middle
Is it fair to say that that's
what's driving the higher sense

14:39.846 --> 14:41.216 align:middle
of upside risk on inflation?

14:41.326 --> 14:41.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:41.616 --> 14:43.866 align:middle
Actually, that's not the
only thing that's happened.

14:43.866 --> 14:46.176 align:middle
What's happened is that
our forecast for inflation

14:46.176 --> 14:48.706 align:middle
for this year, I think,
are five-tenths higher

14:48.706 --> 14:49.676 align:middle
than they were in September.

14:50.176 --> 14:52.696 align:middle
So, you've got-you had two
months of higher inflation,

14:52.696 --> 14:53.706 align:middle
September and October.

14:54.276 --> 14:57.376 align:middle
As I mentioned, November
is, is back on track.

14:57.376 --> 14:59.866 align:middle
But, you know, once
again we've, you know,

15:00.126 --> 15:03.836 align:middle
we've had a year-end projection
for inflation and it's kind

15:03.836 --> 15:05.746 align:middle
of fallen apart as we've
approached the end of the year.

15:05.746 --> 15:10.136 align:middle
So that is certainly a large
factor in people's thinking.

15:10.136 --> 15:12.446 align:middle
I can tell you that might be
the single biggest factor is

15:12.886 --> 15:15.776 align:middle
inflation has once again
underperformed relative

15:15.776 --> 15:16.686 align:middle
to expectations.

15:16.686 --> 15:20.396 align:middle
It's still, you know, going to
be between 2½ and 3 [percent].

15:20.396 --> 15:21.966 align:middle
It's way below where it was.

15:21.966 --> 15:25.886 align:middle
But, you know, we really want to
see progress on inflation, to,

15:25.946 --> 15:28.906 align:middle
to, you know, as I mentioned,
as we think about further cuts,

15:28.906 --> 15:30.846 align:middle
we're going to be looking
for progress on inflation.

15:31.136 --> 15:34.226 align:middle
We have been moving sideways
on 12-month inflation

15:34.226 --> 15:35.756 align:middle
as the 12-month window moves.

15:36.226 --> 15:40.356 align:middle
That's in part because inflation
was very, very low measured in,

15:40.356 --> 15:42.086 align:middle
in the 4th quarter of 2023.

15:42.086 --> 15:43.836 align:middle
Nonetheless, as we go
forward, we're going to want

15:43.836 --> 15:46.576 align:middle
to be seeing further progress
on bringing inflation down

15:46.956 --> 15:48.426 align:middle
and keeping a solid
labor market.

15:48.426 --> 15:49.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:49.366 --> 15:53.656 align:middle
Chris. CHRIS RUGABER.

15:53.696 --> 15:54.316 align:middle
Thank you.

15:54.316 --> 15:55.756 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

15:55.756 --> 15:56.696 align:middle
Thanks for taking the question.

15:56.696 --> 15:59.716 align:middle
In September 2018, the Fed staff

15:59.716 --> 16:01.506 align:middle
in the Tealbook discussed
a policy

16:01.576 --> 16:03.676 align:middle
of "looking through"
any new tariffs as long

16:03.676 --> 16:05.246 align:middle
as they were one-time increases

16:05.246 --> 16:07.146 align:middle
and inflation expectations
remained anchored.

16:07.836 --> 16:10.456 align:middle
Could you comment on if that
analysis remains effective

16:10.856 --> 16:12.826 align:middle
and any other thinking

16:12.826 --> 16:14.516 align:middle
on tariffs generally
that you can share?

16:14.756 --> 16:15.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:15.236 --> 16:17.266 align:middle
So I do think that the 2018,

16:17.266 --> 16:21.296 align:middle
the September 2018 Tealbook
alternative simulations are a

16:21.296 --> 16:22.216 align:middle
good place to start.

16:22.216 --> 16:23.696 align:middle
I happened to have brought
them here with me today.

16:24.386 --> 16:26.196 align:middle
I'm sure you have them too.

16:26.626 --> 16:28.126 align:middle
They're, they're a good
starting point, and,

16:28.236 --> 16:31.516 align:middle
and I would just say, you know,
it's six-years-old analysis.

16:31.516 --> 16:33.796 align:middle
But nonetheless, this is still,
I think, I think the right,

16:33.956 --> 16:35.296 align:middle
the right questions to ask.

16:35.296 --> 16:37.796 align:middle
And, you know, there,
there were two simulations:

16:37.796 --> 16:41.146 align:middle
one was-one was "seeing
through," one was not.

16:41.656 --> 16:43.926 align:middle
And I point you to, you'll-
there's some language

16:43.926 --> 16:46.976 align:middle
in the-in the "see-through"
paragraph.

16:46.976 --> 16:50.016 align:middle
It says, you know, it
considers situations

16:50.016 --> 16:53.736 align:middle
in which it might be appropriate
to "see through" inflation

16:53.736 --> 16:55.706 align:middle
and then, and then names
some conditions that in,

16:55.706 --> 16:56.786 align:middle
in which it might not be.

16:56.966 --> 16:58.926 align:middle
In any case, this is
not a question that's

16:58.926 --> 17:00.736 align:middle
in front of us right now.

17:00.826 --> 17:03.506 align:middle
We, we won't face that
question-we don't know

17:03.506 --> 17:04.636 align:middle
when we'll face that question.

17:04.986 --> 17:09.006 align:middle
What the Committee's doing
now is, is discussing pathways

17:09.006 --> 17:10.306 align:middle
and understanding, again,

17:10.306 --> 17:16.166 align:middle
the ways in which tariff-driven
inflation can affect-tariffs can

17:16.166 --> 17:18.496 align:middle
affect inflation and
the economy, and,

17:18.496 --> 17:20.316 align:middle
and how to think about that.

17:20.316 --> 17:23.056 align:middle
So, we're, we're-we've done
a bit of, good bit of work,

17:23.056 --> 17:25.806 align:middle
all of us have, each of
us has, and, you know,

17:25.806 --> 17:28.896 align:middle
it puts us in position, when
we finally do see what the,

17:29.066 --> 17:32.776 align:middle
what the actual policies are, to
be, to make, you know, a more,

17:33.406 --> 17:34.976 align:middle
more careful, thoughtful
assessment

17:34.976 --> 17:36.946 align:middle
of what might be the
appropriate policy response.

17:37.246 --> 17:37.786 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

17:37.786 --> 17:38.856 align:middle
Great, and just a
quick follow[-up].

17:38.856 --> 17:41.636 align:middle
Do you, given the
recent bout of inflation

17:41.636 --> 17:44.226 align:middle
that we've been through, with
consumers seeing how, you know,

17:44.226 --> 17:46.856 align:middle
prices can rise and
businesses being-seeing

17:46.856 --> 17:50.396 align:middle
that they can raise prices,
at least for some time,

17:50.886 --> 17:55.846 align:middle
does that make it a little bit
riskier to look through tariffs?

17:55.846 --> 17:58.826 align:middle
I mean, are-do you feel that
you have to respond more quickly

17:58.826 --> 18:02.346 align:middle
to inflation threats, given what
we've seen the past few years?

18:02.346 --> 18:02.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:02.836 --> 18:04.956 align:middle
So the, the main thing
is-and this is also a point

18:04.956 --> 18:06.706 align:middle
in these alternative
simulations-is

18:06.796 --> 18:11.056 align:middle
that there're just many, many
factors that go into what,

18:11.056 --> 18:16.026 align:middle
what-how much tariffs will, will
even go into consumer inflation.

18:16.466 --> 18:17.886 align:middle
How persistent will that be?

18:18.326 --> 18:21.396 align:middle
And, you know, so, we just-we
just don't know, really,

18:21.586 --> 18:23.666 align:middle
very much at all about
the actual policies,

18:23.666 --> 18:26.966 align:middle
so it's very premature to try
to make any kind of conclusion.

18:26.966 --> 18:29.276 align:middle
We don't know what'll be
tariffed, from what countries,

18:29.636 --> 18:31.416 align:middle
for how long, and what size.

18:31.416 --> 18:32.366 align:middle
We don't know whether
there'll be,

18:32.366 --> 18:34.666 align:middle
you know, retaliatory tariffs.

18:34.666 --> 18:36.936 align:middle
We don't know what the, you
know, the transmission of any

18:36.936 --> 18:38.886 align:middle
of that will be into
consumer prices.

18:38.946 --> 18:44.716 align:middle
To your-to your point as
well, I don't-I wouldn't say

18:44.716 --> 18:47.426 align:middle
that we know whether
the last episode is

18:47.426 --> 18:50.326 align:middle
or is not a good model
for what happened.

18:50.326 --> 18:51.486 align:middle
You pointed out we've just been

18:51.486 --> 18:53.626 align:middle
through a period
of high inflation.

18:53.626 --> 18:55.036 align:middle
We've just gotten
through that period.

18:55.496 --> 18:56.366 align:middle
That's a difference.

18:56.856 --> 18:59.786 align:middle
It was also quite a bit
of diversion of trade away

18:59.786 --> 19:01.706 align:middle
from China to other
countries, since that,

19:02.096 --> 19:03.846 align:middle
that may-that may have affects.

19:03.846 --> 19:04.196 align:middle
I don't know.

19:04.196 --> 19:07.576 align:middle
I just think we, we need
to take our time, not rush,

19:08.036 --> 19:10.116 align:middle
and make a very careful
assessment,

19:10.156 --> 19:12.526 align:middle
but only when we've actually
seen what the policies are

19:12.526 --> 19:13.696 align:middle
and how they're implemented.

19:14.196 --> 19:16.196 align:middle
And, you know, we're just-we're
just not at that stage.

19:16.196 --> 19:18.876 align:middle
We're at the stage of doing
what other forecasters are

19:18.876 --> 19:21.436 align:middle
doing-which is kind of thinking
about these questions, but not,

19:21.436 --> 19:24.976 align:middle
not trying to get to definitive
answers for some time.

19:26.006 --> 19:26.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:26.426 --> 19:29.596 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

19:30.166 --> 19:31.496 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

19:31.576 --> 19:33.046 align:middle
Chair Powell, to make
sure I understand,

19:33.136 --> 19:34.526 align:middle
participants today revised

19:34.526 --> 19:37.526 align:middle
up their core PCE
inflation projection for '25

19:37.526 --> 19:41.426 align:middle
so that the central tendency
runs from 2½ to 2.7 percent,

19:41.936 --> 19:43.036 align:middle
and, as Howard noted earlier,

19:43.036 --> 19:44.676 align:middle
most of the Committee
sees the risks

19:44.676 --> 19:46.276 align:middle
to those projections
to the upside.

19:46.896 --> 19:51.826 align:middle
So if inflation only declines
next year from 2.8 to 2.5

19:51.826 --> 19:54.706 align:middle
to 2.7, what would
compel the Committee

19:54.706 --> 19:57.216 align:middle
to be cutting in that situation?

19:57.216 --> 19:59.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:59.366 --> 20:00.746 align:middle
Let me-let me find
these numbers.

20:03.926 --> 20:12.856 align:middle
So we have-we have
inflation coming down,

20:13.136 --> 20:15.326 align:middle
core inflation coming down
to 2½ [percent] next year.

20:15.896 --> 20:19.556 align:middle
That's, that'd be-that'd
be significant progress.

20:19.556 --> 20:20.696 align:middle
You see a slower path.

20:20.916 --> 20:22.666 align:middle
I think that does take on board

20:22.666 --> 20:24.086 align:middle
that we want to see
real progress.

20:24.136 --> 20:26.856 align:middle
But we, we, you know, we'd
be seeing meaningful progress

20:26.856 --> 20:29.396 align:middle
to get inflation
down to that level.

20:29.396 --> 20:31.576 align:middle
That wouldn't be all the way to
2 percent, but that would be,

20:31.576 --> 20:33.276 align:middle
it would be, you know,
better than this year.

20:33.366 --> 20:35.976 align:middle
This year, [it] will
be 2.8 or 2.9.

20:35.976 --> 20:36.766 align:middle
That would be meaningful.

20:36.766 --> 20:38.146 align:middle
We, we also have to
think about the, the,

20:38.146 --> 20:39.656 align:middle
you know, the labor market.

20:39.766 --> 20:42.296 align:middle
And while we, we have the
labor market forecast as being

20:43.016 --> 20:46.176 align:middle
in good shape, we are also
mindful that it is still

20:46.466 --> 20:48.386 align:middle
out there very gradually
cooling,

20:48.426 --> 20:51.056 align:middle
so far in an orderly,
gradual way.

20:51.566 --> 20:53.336 align:middle
But it's also something we
need to keep our eye on.

20:53.626 --> 20:54.116 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

20:54.116 --> 20:55.986 align:middle
I, I guess, if I could
follow up: If somebody looked

20:55.986 --> 20:59.556 align:middle
at these projections and also
the insertion of the "extent

20:59.556 --> 21:01.896 align:middle
and timing" language in the
statement, which has been used

21:01.896 --> 21:04.656 align:middle
at times in the past when the
Committee thinks maybe it's

21:04.656 --> 21:07.626 align:middle
going to be on hold for a
while, and they said, "Gee,

21:07.626 --> 21:10.306 align:middle
this looks like it could be the
last rate cut for some time,"

21:10.796 --> 21:12.156 align:middle
would they be mistaken
to, to infer that?

21:12.156 --> 21:13.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:13.786 --> 21:17.856 align:middle
So that's, that's not
the-that's not any decision

21:17.856 --> 21:18.656 align:middle
that we've made at all.

21:18.656 --> 21:20.606 align:middle
Let me explain "extent
and timing."

21:20.606 --> 21:23.426 align:middle
So the, the sense of that
wording is to make clear

21:23.426 --> 21:26.486 align:middle
that if the economy does
evolve about as anticipated,

21:26.916 --> 21:28.776 align:middle
we're at a point at which
it would be appropriate

21:28.776 --> 21:30.776 align:middle
to slow the pace of rate cuts.

21:30.776 --> 21:32.616 align:middle
So "extent," that
just relates to how,

21:32.766 --> 21:35.466 align:middle
how much further we can reduce
our policy rate consistent

21:35.466 --> 21:36.756 align:middle
with getting to a
neutral stance.

21:37.106 --> 21:39.796 align:middle
Clearly, that distance has
shrunk by 100 basis points.

21:40.326 --> 21:41.976 align:middle
So it's significantly smaller.

21:42.526 --> 21:44.446 align:middle
So that's, that's the
"extent" question.

21:44.576 --> 21:46.936 align:middle
And, again, we're going to be
looking for further progress

21:46.936 --> 21:47.996 align:middle
on inflation, as well

21:47.996 --> 21:49.986 align:middle
as a strong labor market,
to make those cuts.

21:50.316 --> 21:53.866 align:middle
"Timing" just suggests, again,
that we're at a place where we,

21:53.866 --> 21:58.126 align:middle
we, assuming the economy
develops as expected, we're at

21:58.126 --> 22:00.376 align:middle
or near a level that
will make it appropriate

22:00.376 --> 22:01.616 align:middle
to slow the pace of adjustments.

22:01.616 --> 22:02.956 align:middle
So that, that's what
we mean by that.

22:03.326 --> 22:06.426 align:middle
We're not trying to make
decisions about the longer run.

22:06.546 --> 22:08.176 align:middle
You know, we are, we're trying

22:08.176 --> 22:10.546 align:middle
to make sensible
policy as we go.

22:11.166 --> 22:13.426 align:middle
And, you know, I just would
emphasize the uncertainty,

22:13.426 --> 22:16.176 align:middle
which is, it's just-it's just
a function of the fact that,

22:16.306 --> 22:18.406 align:middle
that we expect significant
policy changes.

22:18.406 --> 22:21.346 align:middle
There's nothing really
unusual about that.

22:21.346 --> 22:23.106 align:middle
I think we need to, we
need to see what they are

22:23.106 --> 22:24.616 align:middle
and see what the
effects they will have.

22:24.616 --> 22:26.386 align:middle
We'll, we'll have a much
clearer picture, I think,

22:26.936 --> 22:28.396 align:middle
you know, when that happens.

22:29.466 --> 22:29.936 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:30.336 --> 22:32.866 align:middle
Mike. MICHAEL MCKEE.

22:33.976 --> 22:36.376 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

22:36.376 --> 22:41.226 align:middle
Even though you've cut rates
by 100 basis points this year,

22:41.226 --> 22:43.466 align:middle
we haven't seen much
change in mortgages,

22:43.466 --> 22:47.196 align:middle
auto loan rates, or
credit card rates.

22:47.526 --> 22:49.456 align:middle
You say you're significantly
restrictive.

22:49.886 --> 22:52.906 align:middle
Are you running a risk that the
markets are fighting against you

22:52.946 --> 22:56.316 align:middle
and the economy could
be more at risk

22:56.316 --> 22:58.296 align:middle
of a slowdown than
you anticipate?

22:58.546 --> 22:59.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:59.456 --> 23:03.266 align:middle
So the, the risks
that you-sorry,

23:03.266 --> 23:04.936 align:middle
the rates that you
talked about are,

23:04.936 --> 23:06.896 align:middle
are really longer-run
rates, and they're affected,

23:06.976 --> 23:09.046 align:middle
they are affected to some
extent by Fed policy,

23:09.046 --> 23:11.186 align:middle
but they're also affected
by many other things.

23:11.746 --> 23:14.776 align:middle
And longer rates have
actually gone up quite a bit

23:14.906 --> 23:17.686 align:middle
since September, as, as you well
know, and those are the things

23:17.686 --> 23:20.256 align:middle
that drive, for example,
mortgage rates more

23:20.256 --> 23:22.036 align:middle
than short-term rates do.

23:22.806 --> 23:27.226 align:middle
So we look at that, but we look
at all financial conditions,

23:27.226 --> 23:29.066 align:middle
and then we look at what's
happening in the economy.

23:29.066 --> 23:32.986 align:middle
So what we see happening in the
economy, again, is, you know,

23:32.986 --> 23:36.006 align:middle
most forecasters have been
calling for a slowdown in growth

23:36.006 --> 23:38.446 align:middle
for a very long time, and
it keeps not happening.

23:38.856 --> 23:42.146 align:middle
So we have-we're now well
into another year of growth.

23:42.146 --> 23:44.026 align:middle
It looks like it
might be 2½ percent.

23:44.726 --> 23:47.036 align:middle
Third and-second and
third quarters were right

23:47.036 --> 23:47.936 align:middle
about at the same level.

23:47.936 --> 23:51.596 align:middle
So the U.S. economy is just
performing very, very well.

23:51.736 --> 23:54.516 align:middle
Substantially better than
our global peer group.

23:55.216 --> 23:58.626 align:middle
And I, I-there's no reason
to think a downturn is,

23:58.726 --> 24:01.936 align:middle
is any more likely than
it-than it usually is.

24:02.416 --> 24:06.066 align:middle
So, the outlook is pretty
bright for our economy.

24:06.066 --> 24:09.186 align:middle
We have to stay on task,
though, and continue

24:09.186 --> 24:10.306 align:middle
to have restrictive policies

24:10.306 --> 24:12.276 align:middle
so that we can get
inflation down to 2 percent.

24:12.796 --> 24:14.606 align:middle
We're also going to be looking
out for the labor market.

24:14.606 --> 24:16.896 align:middle
We want to keep the labor market
pretty close to where it is.

24:17.366 --> 24:19.926 align:middle
We're pretty close
to estimates of the,

24:19.986 --> 24:21.676 align:middle
the natural rate
of unemployment.

24:22.476 --> 24:24.996 align:middle
Job creation is, is
a little below the,

24:25.226 --> 24:27.786 align:middle
the level that would keep it
there but, nonetheless, close.

24:28.386 --> 24:31.196 align:middle
And so that's what our
policy's trying to achieve.

24:31.196 --> 24:31.546 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

24:31.826 --> 24:34.886 align:middle
If I could follow up by
asking about-your formulation

24:34.886 --> 24:38.736 align:middle
for beginning rate cuts
included the phrase "we need

24:39.066 --> 24:41.916 align:middle
to have confidence that
inflation is moving

24:41.976 --> 24:43.356 align:middle
down towards our target."

24:43.446 --> 24:45.716 align:middle
Given the fact that
you've raised your forecast

24:45.716 --> 24:49.366 align:middle
for next year, do you have
confidence or are you uncertain

24:49.656 --> 24:51.796 align:middle
about the path of
inflation going forward?

24:51.796 --> 24:52.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:52.026 --> 24:55.086 align:middle
So confidence was our test for
raising rates, and, you know,

24:55.086 --> 24:57.396 align:middle
we've made-look, look
at the broader suite:

24:57.396 --> 24:59.366 align:middle
We've made just a
great deal of progress.

24:59.366 --> 25:04.496 align:middle
We're, you know, we're well into
the 2's in, in core inflation

25:04.496 --> 25:06.476 align:middle
and around 2½ [percent]-or
even lower than that,

25:06.476 --> 25:09.376 align:middle
we have been-for
headline inflation.

25:09.756 --> 25:14.006 align:middle
So I, I would say I'm confident
that inflation has come

25:14.006 --> 25:16.626 align:middle
down a great deal, and I,
I'm confident in the story

25:17.196 --> 25:20.136 align:middle
about why it's come down
and why that portends well.

25:20.176 --> 25:21.456 align:middle
And I'll-and I'll tell you why.

25:21.976 --> 25:25.116 align:middle
You, again, you do see with
housing services inflation,

25:25.116 --> 25:27.056 align:middle
which is one that we've
really worried, worried about,

25:27.296 --> 25:30.686 align:middle
it really has come down now
quite steadily, at a slower pace

25:30.686 --> 25:32.486 align:middle
than we thought, you
know, two years ago,

25:32.576 --> 25:37.086 align:middle
but it's nonetheless steadily
coming down as, as market rents,

25:37.376 --> 25:41.686 align:middle
you know, market rents start
to equilibrate better with,

25:42.346 --> 25:45.356 align:middle
you know, new leases
that turn over.

25:45.486 --> 25:47.376 align:middle
Not, not new tenants,
but new leases.

25:47.376 --> 25:48.646 align:middle
Market rents is new leases.

25:49.166 --> 25:50.816 align:middle
So that, that's happening;

25:50.816 --> 25:53.056 align:middle
that process is ongoing
pretty much as we expect.

25:53.446 --> 25:56.406 align:middle
Goods inflation, which is
another big piece of it,

25:56.916 --> 25:58.536 align:middle
has returned right to the range

25:58.536 --> 25:59.936 align:middle
where it was before
the pandemic.

26:00.286 --> 26:02.876 align:middle
Just for some months this
year it kind of moved

26:02.876 --> 26:05.596 align:middle
up in a bumpy way because of
used cars and things like that.

26:05.596 --> 26:09.106 align:middle
But we think, overall,
that should generally be

26:09.106 --> 26:09.976 align:middle
in the range it was in.

26:09.976 --> 26:12.236 align:middle
Then that leaves
nonhousing services,

26:12.676 --> 26:17.456 align:middle
and market-based nonhousing
services are in, in good shape.

26:17.456 --> 26:20.626 align:middle
It's nonmarket services, and
those are-those are services

26:20.626 --> 26:25.166 align:middle
that are imputed rather than
measured directly, and they,

26:25.166 --> 26:27.376 align:middle
they don't, we, we think they
don't really tell us much

26:27.446 --> 26:31.086 align:middle
about the, you know, about
tightness in the economy.

26:31.086 --> 26:32.196 align:middle
They don't really reflect that.

26:32.776 --> 26:35.076 align:middle
I mean, a good example
is financial services,

26:35.076 --> 26:37.026 align:middle
which is really done
off of asset prices,

26:37.566 --> 26:40.236 align:middle
and that just-so that, that's
how that inflation works.

26:40.236 --> 26:41.616 align:middle
So the overall picture,

26:41.866 --> 26:44.186 align:middle
the story of why inflation
should be coming down,

26:44.716 --> 26:45.706 align:middle
is still intact.

26:45.706 --> 26:47.906 align:middle
In particular, the labor
market-look at the labor market.

26:47.906 --> 26:51.946 align:middle
It's, it is cooler by so many
measures now-modestly cooler

26:51.946 --> 26:54.216 align:middle
than it was in 2019, a year

26:54.216 --> 26:56.616 align:middle
when inflation was
well under 2 percent.

26:57.276 --> 26:59.966 align:middle
So it's not a source of
inflationary pressures.

27:00.266 --> 27:03.196 align:middle
Not to say there aren't
regional and, and, you know,

27:03.196 --> 27:05.136 align:middle
particular professions
where labor is tight.

27:05.546 --> 27:08.246 align:middle
But, overall, you're not
getting inflationary impulses

27:08.246 --> 27:09.926 align:middle
of any significance
from the labor market.

27:09.926 --> 27:11.316 align:middle
So what's the story?

27:11.316 --> 27:13.716 align:middle
The story is still
just, we're unwinding

27:13.716 --> 27:18.856 align:middle
from these large shocks that the
economy got in 2021 and '22 in,

27:18.856 --> 27:22.706 align:middle
for example, housing services,
and now also in, in insurance,

27:22.706 --> 27:25.786 align:middle
in particular, where
costs went up,

27:25.786 --> 27:28.746 align:middle
and those are now
being reflected later

27:28.986 --> 27:29.946 align:middle
in housing insurance.

27:29.946 --> 27:31.006 align:middle
It's real inflation,

27:31.006 --> 27:34.076 align:middle
but it doesn't portend
persistently high inflation.

27:34.076 --> 27:37.396 align:middle
So we and most other forecasters
still feel that we're

27:37.396 --> 27:40.696 align:middle
on track to, you know,
to get down to 2 percent.

27:40.876 --> 27:43.606 align:middle
It might take another year
or two from here, but I,

27:43.646 --> 27:45.376 align:middle
I'm confident that
that's the path we're on.

27:45.376 --> 27:48.926 align:middle
And, you know, our policy will
do everything it can to assure

27:48.926 --> 27:49.896 align:middle
that that is the case.

27:50.016 --> 27:50.766 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:51.446 --> 27:55.856 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

27:58.006 --> 27:58.326 align:middle
Thank you.

27:58.326 --> 27:59.736 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

28:00.106 --> 28:02.686 align:middle
So the unemployment rate as of
November, while still very low,

28:02.686 --> 28:05.476 align:middle
is within spitting distance of
the level that generated a lot

28:05.476 --> 28:08.236 align:middle
of concern about the labor
market over the summer

28:08.636 --> 28:11.006 align:middle
in the lead-up to the 50
basis point cut in September.

28:11.276 --> 28:13.956 align:middle
Hiring has also narrowed to
just a handful of sectors.

28:14.396 --> 28:17.036 align:middle
But now the Committee appears
comfortable skipping cuts

28:17.036 --> 28:17.996 align:middle
at upcoming meetings.

28:18.066 --> 28:20.746 align:middle
So what has changed about
the Committee's assessment

28:20.746 --> 28:22.666 align:middle
of the risks confronting
the labor market?

28:22.926 --> 28:27.096 align:middle
Is there just less concern now
on that front, or is it just

28:27.096 --> 28:29.366 align:middle
about there being more
upside risk to inflation

28:29.586 --> 28:31.016 align:middle
that now needs to
be accounted for?

28:31.536 --> 28:32.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:32.406 --> 28:35.386 align:middle
The, the unemployment rate
has now-is now the same

28:35.386 --> 28:37.376 align:middle
as it was in July: 4.2 percent.

28:37.376 --> 28:40.966 align:middle
It's moved up and down, but
it's now the same as July.

28:41.406 --> 28:46.316 align:middle
And job creation is, is
lower than it has been,

28:46.316 --> 28:48.846 align:middle
but it's been-it's been
steady-it's not-it's

28:48.846 --> 28:49.606 align:middle
not declining.

28:49.606 --> 28:52.096 align:middle
It's been steady at a
level which, as I pointed

28:52.096 --> 28:54.406 align:middle
out a couple times, is
actually below the level

28:54.506 --> 28:56.286 align:middle
that would hold the
unemployment rate constant,

28:56.286 --> 28:57.616 align:middle
but it's not so far below.

28:58.036 --> 29:01.206 align:middle
So, you might-you
might-if, if we have the,

29:01.276 --> 29:02.386 align:middle
the breakeven level right,

29:02.386 --> 29:05.726 align:middle
and if jobs continue-job
creation continues at that level

29:05.726 --> 29:08.006 align:middle
in the establishment survey,

29:08.156 --> 29:11.656 align:middle
then you would get a tenth maybe
every other month kind of thing.

29:11.656 --> 29:13.176 align:middle
So gradually declining.

29:13.176 --> 29:14.866 align:middle
But we don't have that
kind of precision in this.

29:14.866 --> 29:16.756 align:middle
So, but you're right,
though, and I,

29:16.756 --> 29:18.026 align:middle
I read out some of the reasons.

29:18.026 --> 29:20.576 align:middle
We, we do think the labor
market is still cooling

29:20.916 --> 29:23.846 align:middle
by many measures, and we're
watching that closely.

29:24.076 --> 29:30.076 align:middle
It's, it's not cooling in
a-in a quick or in a way

29:30.076 --> 29:31.496 align:middle
that really raises concerns.

29:31.496 --> 29:35.876 align:middle
I think, you know, you
pointed out participants

29:35.876 --> 29:37.646 align:middle
in the FOMC really
thought that the risks

29:37.646 --> 29:40.376 align:middle
and uncertainty had improved
relative to the labor market.

29:40.376 --> 29:43.416 align:middle
And it's because of, because
things have just gotten a little

29:43.416 --> 29:45.486 align:middle
bit better, it doesn't-the
unemployment rate flat

29:45.486 --> 29:46.206 align:middle
and things like that.

29:46.206 --> 29:48.126 align:middle
Nonetheless, you know,
we're watching it closely.

29:48.806 --> 29:49.466 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

29:49.466 --> 29:52.686 align:middle
If, if the idea, though, is that
no additional kind of softening

29:52.686 --> 29:55.416 align:middle
in the labor market is
welcome here, what's to prevent

29:55.416 --> 29:57.686 align:middle
that from happening if
rates are still restrictive?

29:58.116 --> 29:58.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:58.796 --> 30:02.086 align:middle
So I-what I said is, we don't
think we need further softening

30:02.086 --> 30:03.386 align:middle
to get to 2 percent inflation.

30:03.876 --> 30:06.076 align:middle
That's what I'm saying,
not that it's not welcome.

30:06.076 --> 30:09.086 align:middle
We don't need it,
we don't think.

30:09.086 --> 30:11.946 align:middle
If, if you had a situation
where inflation's moving

30:11.946 --> 30:14.936 align:middle
around by a tenth every few
months, that's, you know,

30:14.936 --> 30:18.296 align:middle
we'd have to weigh that against
the fact that inflation has

30:18.706 --> 30:22.266 align:middle
in recent months been moving
sideways in the 12-month window.

30:22.886 --> 30:25.556 align:middle
And so we've got to weigh
them both, at this point.

30:25.556 --> 30:28.536 align:middle
You know, for a while there we
were only really focused mainly,

30:28.606 --> 30:30.316 align:middle
mainly focused on, on inflation.

30:30.826 --> 30:34.196 align:middle
We've now gotten to a place
where, where the risks

30:34.196 --> 30:37.116 align:middle
of the two are what we think
are broadly, roughly in balance.

30:37.776 --> 30:38.866 align:middle
And so that's how
we think about it.

30:38.926 --> 30:39.856 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:39.996 --> 30:42.136 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

30:43.596 --> 30:45.996 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I, I did
not hear you use the word

30:46.036 --> 30:47.146 align:middle
"recalibration" today.

30:47.736 --> 30:51.076 align:middle
And I'm just wondering if the
recalibration phase is over

30:51.686 --> 30:55.486 align:middle
and what you might call this new
phase and whether the criteria

30:55.486 --> 30:58.406 align:middle
for changing rates
is somehow different

30:58.406 --> 30:59.676 align:middle
and higher than it was before.

30:59.716 --> 31:01.536 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

31:02.026 --> 31:06.216 align:middle
We're not-we're not
renaming the phase-yet.

31:06.926 --> 31:08.066 align:middle
But we may get around to that.

31:08.176 --> 31:12.016 align:middle
But, no, I, I would say we, we
are, though, in, in a new phase

31:12.016 --> 31:13.516 align:middle
in the process, as I said.

31:13.516 --> 31:17.326 align:middle
So, and that's just because
we've reduced-we've reduced our

31:17.326 --> 31:19.516 align:middle
policy rate by 100 basis points.

31:19.516 --> 31:21.136 align:middle
We're significantly
closer to neutral.

31:21.506 --> 31:23.996 align:middle
We still think where
we are is meaningfully,

31:23.996 --> 31:27.526 align:middle
meaningfully restrictive, and I
think from this point forward,

31:28.026 --> 31:31.086 align:middle
you know, it's appropriate
to move cautiously and look

31:31.086 --> 31:32.566 align:middle
for progress on inflation.

31:33.086 --> 31:35.986 align:middle
We've, you know, we've done a
lot to support economic activity

31:36.046 --> 31:40.876 align:middle
by cutting 100 basis points,
and that's a good thing.

31:41.156 --> 31:43.516 align:middle
I, I think it would-I
support the decision, and,

31:43.516 --> 31:44.956 align:middle
and I think it was the
right decision to make.

31:45.276 --> 31:48.846 align:middle
I think from now we are in a
place where the risks really are

31:48.846 --> 31:51.806 align:middle
in balance, and we need to
see progress on inflation.

31:52.056 --> 31:54.036 align:middle
And that's, that's how
we're thinking about it.

31:54.036 --> 31:55.266 align:middle
So it is kind of a new thing.

31:55.266 --> 31:57.296 align:middle
We moved pretty quickly
to get to here,

31:57.436 --> 31:59.016 align:middle
and I think going
forward, obviously,

31:59.016 --> 31:59.866 align:middle
we're going to be moving slower,

31:59.866 --> 32:02.256 align:middle
which is consistent
with, with the SEP.

32:02.256 --> 32:03.026 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

32:03.296 --> 32:05.686 align:middle
Wonder if I could follow
up and ask you how much you

32:05.686 --> 32:08.476 align:middle
or the Committee are
looking through some

32:08.516 --> 32:11.226 align:middle
of the high numbers we've had
in the recent inflation numbers.

32:11.226 --> 32:14.726 align:middle
For example, cars being up
maybe because of the hurricanes,

32:14.726 --> 32:17.776 align:middle
eggs because of the avian
flu, that, that kind of stuff.

32:17.846 --> 32:19.896 align:middle
And, and then looking
forward, perhaps,

32:20.116 --> 32:22.306 align:middle
to housing inflation
coming down,

32:22.306 --> 32:23.566 align:middle
as it did in the recent report.

32:23.566 --> 32:24.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:24.466 --> 32:27.536 align:middle
So we, yeah, we always try to
be careful about not throwing

32:27.536 --> 32:29.046 align:middle
out the numbers we
don't like, you know?

32:29.046 --> 32:32.696 align:middle
It's just a, an occupational
hazard is, is to [say], "Look,

32:32.696 --> 32:33.896 align:middle
those high months are wrong."

32:33.896 --> 32:34.736 align:middle
What about the low months?

32:34.736 --> 32:36.576 align:middle
You know, we've got, we
have a very low month,

32:36.576 --> 32:37.776 align:middle
potentially, in November.

32:38.746 --> 32:42.026 align:middle
You know, it's estimated
by many to be

32:42.026 --> 32:44.456 align:middle
in the mid-teens for core PCE.

32:45.036 --> 32:46.886 align:middle
So that could be
idiosyncratically low.

32:47.166 --> 32:48.446 align:middle
We try to look-we try to look

32:48.446 --> 32:50.286 align:middle
at not just a couple
or three months.

32:50.286 --> 32:53.176 align:middle
We shouldn't have-our, our
position shouldn't change based

32:53.176 --> 32:55.436 align:middle
on two or three months
of good or bad data.

32:55.826 --> 32:58.336 align:middle
We have a long string
now of inflation coming

32:58.336 --> 33:00.876 align:middle
down gradually over time.

33:01.026 --> 33:02.366 align:middle
As I mentioned, 12-month,

33:02.426 --> 33:05.836 align:middle
I think it's 12-month headline's
2½; 12-month core is 2.8.

33:06.166 --> 33:07.586 align:middle
That's way better than we were.

33:08.166 --> 33:09.366 align:middle
We still have some
work to do, though,

33:09.586 --> 33:10.776 align:middle
is how we're looking at it.

33:10.826 --> 33:14.146 align:middle
And we need policy to remain
restrictive to, to get-to get

33:14.146 --> 33:15.116 align:middle
that work done, we think.

33:17.156 --> 33:19.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:19.696 --> 33:20.936 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

33:20.936 --> 33:22.526 align:middle
Thanks Chair, Chair Powell.

33:22.706 --> 33:24.446 align:middle
Neil Irwin from Axios.

33:24.446 --> 33:26.936 align:middle
Financial markets have been
very buoyant, really all year.

33:26.936 --> 33:28.186 align:middle
Is the Committee comfortable

33:28.186 --> 33:30.726 align:middle
with where financial conditions
are, or do you see a risk

33:30.726 --> 33:32.846 align:middle
that looseness in financial
conditions could undermine

33:32.846 --> 33:34.186 align:middle
progress on your
inflation target?

33:34.316 --> 33:34.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:34.816 --> 33:37.546 align:middle
So we, we do look carefully at
financial conditions, of course;

33:37.546 --> 33:38.496 align:middle
that's part of what we do.

33:38.496 --> 33:40.386 align:middle
But, you know, we, we
really look carefully

33:40.386 --> 33:44.526 align:middle
at is the performance of
our-of our goal variables and,

33:44.526 --> 33:46.116 align:middle
and how are we affecting
the economy.

33:46.116 --> 33:49.086 align:middle
So what we've seen over
the course of-just take the

33:49.086 --> 33:49.726 align:middle
last year.

33:50.316 --> 33:53.716 align:middle
We, we've seen inflation-well,
over the last couple

33:53.716 --> 33:54.826 align:middle
of years-come down a lot.

33:55.106 --> 33:57.796 align:middle
We've seen the labor
market cool off quite a bit.

33:58.126 --> 34:00.096 align:middle
That suggests that our
policy is restrictive.

34:00.626 --> 34:03.736 align:middle
We've, we can also look more
directly at, at the parts

34:03.736 --> 34:06.126 align:middle
of the economy that
are affected by-that,

34:06.216 --> 34:09.056 align:middle
that are interest sensitive,
like particularly housing.

34:09.446 --> 34:13.336 align:middle
Housing activity is, is
very low, and that's partly,

34:13.536 --> 34:15.256 align:middle
significantly because
of our policy.

34:15.256 --> 34:16.666 align:middle
So we think our policy
is working.

34:16.666 --> 34:18.486 align:middle
It's transmitting, and
it's having the effects

34:18.486 --> 34:21.506 align:middle
on our goal variables
that we would want.

34:21.876 --> 34:24.206 align:middle
You know, a lot of things move
financial conditions around,

34:24.206 --> 34:26.736 align:middle
as you know, and we don't
really control those.

34:26.816 --> 34:29.576 align:middle
But I'd say we, we see the
effects we're hoping to see on,

34:29.676 --> 34:31.996 align:middle
on the goal variables
and, and the places

34:31.996 --> 34:32.796 align:middle
where we'd expect to see it.

34:32.796 --> 34:33.246 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

34:33.486 --> 34:35.216 align:middle
So, if I may, speaking of assets

34:35.216 --> 34:37.946 align:middle
that have-that have been
buoyant, do you see any value

34:37.946 --> 34:39.906 align:middle
or benefit in the U.S.
government building a reserve

34:39.906 --> 34:40.486 align:middle
of Bitcoin?

34:42.206 --> 34:42.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:43.266 --> 34:47.456 align:middle
So, you know, we, we're
not allowed to own Bitcoin.

34:47.986 --> 34:50.226 align:middle
The Federal Reserve Act says
what we can own, and we're,

34:50.296 --> 34:51.976 align:middle
we're not looking
for a, a law change.

34:52.216 --> 34:54.176 align:middle
That's the kind of thing
for Congress to consider,

34:54.176 --> 34:57.016 align:middle
but we are not looking for
a law change at the Fed.

34:59.156 --> 34:59.576 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:02.056 --> 35:03.066 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

35:03.146 --> 35:04.886 align:middle
Hey, happy holidays,
Mr. Chairman.

35:04.886 --> 35:06.246 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

35:06.696 --> 35:09.136 align:middle
I was wondering if
you are satisfied

35:09.226 --> 35:12.986 align:middle
with the way 2024 is
ending, if you're confident

35:13.036 --> 35:18.256 align:middle
that we've avoided the recession
that forecasters were predicting

35:18.296 --> 35:19.666 align:middle
as inevitable a couple
years ago.

35:19.886 --> 35:21.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:21.326 --> 35:23.616 align:middle
I think it's pretty clear
we've avoided a recession.

35:23.806 --> 35:27.426 align:middle
I think growth this year has,
has been solid, it really has.

35:28.196 --> 35:31.256 align:middle
PDFP, private domestic
final purchases, which,

35:31.336 --> 35:33.926 align:middle
which we think is the best
indicator of private demand,

35:33.926 --> 35:36.106 align:middle
is looking to come in around 3
percent [real growth] this year.

35:36.666 --> 35:37.666 align:middle
This is a really good number.

35:38.306 --> 35:40.906 align:middle
Again, the U.S. economy
has just been remarkable.

35:41.146 --> 35:44.786 align:middle
And it's-when we, in these
international meetings

35:44.786 --> 35:47.016 align:middle
that I attend, this
has been the story,

35:47.546 --> 35:49.296 align:middle
is that how well
the U.S. is doing.

35:49.296 --> 35:51.836 align:middle
And if you look around the
world, there's just a lot

35:51.836 --> 35:55.226 align:middle
of slow growth and continued
struggles with inflation.

35:55.966 --> 36:00.216 align:middle
So I feel-I feel very good
about where the economy is

36:00.216 --> 36:01.446 align:middle
and the performance
of the economy,

36:01.446 --> 36:02.336 align:middle
and we want to keep that going.

36:03.016 --> 36:03.536 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

36:03.536 --> 36:05.396 align:middle
The other thing I just wanted
to ask about was the-you,

36:06.226 --> 36:08.746 align:middle
you guys have noted that
the unemployment rate is,

36:09.686 --> 36:10.656 align:middle
is still low.

36:10.656 --> 36:14.586 align:middle
However, employment rates
have fallen rather quickly.

36:14.586 --> 36:17.526 align:middle
The prime-age employment
rate has fell

36:17.526 --> 36:21.226 align:middle
by about half a point-half
a percent, rather-recently.

36:21.226 --> 36:22.856 align:middle
The question, I guess, is,

36:22.856 --> 36:25.586 align:middle
do you think there's maybe
more downside momentum

36:25.586 --> 36:28.576 align:middle
in the labor market than
the unemployment rate alone

36:28.576 --> 36:29.126 align:middle
is signaling?

36:29.406 --> 36:30.256 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:30.256 --> 36:31.046 align:middle
I don't think so, no.

36:31.046 --> 36:32.756 align:middle
I think, overall,
if you look at it,

36:32.826 --> 36:34.996 align:middle
prime-age participation
is still very high.

36:35.586 --> 36:37.936 align:middle
What, what's going on
in the labor market is

36:38.576 --> 36:41.166 align:middle
that the hiring rate is low.

36:41.306 --> 36:44.866 align:middle
So if you-if you have a
job, you're doing very well,

36:45.056 --> 36:46.816 align:middle
and layoffs are very low, right?

36:46.816 --> 36:49.316 align:middle
So people are not losing
their jobs in large numbers,

36:49.316 --> 36:50.356 align:middle
unusually large numbers.

36:50.426 --> 36:54.106 align:middle
If you are looking for a job,
though, the hiring rate is low,

36:54.106 --> 36:57.086 align:middle
and that's a signal of, of lower
demand, and it has come down.

36:57.086 --> 37:01.096 align:middle
So we look for signs like that,
and that, that's clearly a sign

37:01.096 --> 37:02.516 align:middle
of softening, further softening.

37:02.516 --> 37:06.036 align:middle
I didn't mention it earlier,
but I, I think you can see a,

37:06.036 --> 37:08.366 align:middle
an ongoing gradual
softening in the labor market.

37:09.046 --> 37:12.286 align:middle
Again, not something we need to
see to get 2 percent inflation.

37:12.786 --> 37:14.996 align:middle
And, you know, that's
part of the reason

37:14.996 --> 37:17.866 align:middle
that explains why we moved
ahead today with, with our,

37:17.996 --> 37:19.936 align:middle
with the action, with,
with an additional cut.

37:20.436 --> 37:22.286 align:middle
So, but you take a step back,

37:22.286 --> 37:24.826 align:middle
the level of unemployment
is very low-again,

37:24.826 --> 37:25.906 align:middle
participation is high.

37:26.306 --> 37:27.986 align:middle
Wages are at a healthy and,

37:27.986 --> 37:30.936 align:middle
and ever more sustainable
level-wage growth.

37:31.096 --> 37:34.136 align:middle
And so the labor market-this
is a good, a good labor market,

37:34.136 --> 37:35.376 align:middle
and, you know, we want
to keep it that way.

37:36.006 --> 37:37.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:37.106 --> 37:38.536 align:middle
Amara. AMARA OMEOKWE.

37:40.626 --> 37:40.996 align:middle
Thank you.

37:40.996 --> 37:42.376 align:middle
Amara Omeokwe from
Bloomberg News.

37:42.426 --> 37:44.736 align:middle
I just want to put a finer
point on the labor market.

37:45.096 --> 37:48.776 align:middle
Can you keep the labor market
this way, in the strong position

37:48.776 --> 37:51.856 align:middle
that you have described,
without further cuts?

37:51.856 --> 37:53.796 align:middle
In other words, do you
still view the labor market

37:54.156 --> 37:56.876 align:middle
as needing support to protect
against a further cooling?

37:58.536 --> 37:59.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:00.446 --> 38:03.326 align:middle
You know, we, we can't know that
with any tremendous certainty.

38:03.466 --> 38:04.956 align:middle
I, I will say that
we think that,

38:05.016 --> 38:08.466 align:middle
that our policy balances the
risks-we think the risks are

38:08.466 --> 38:10.936 align:middle
roughly in balance as
between the two mandates,

38:11.476 --> 38:14.906 align:middle
and we think the labor
market is, is in solid shape.

38:15.446 --> 38:18.006 align:middle
And when I say it's
softening or cooling,

38:18.106 --> 38:19.796 align:middle
it's very gradual process.

38:20.276 --> 38:22.936 align:middle
You know, job creations are,
are meaningfully positive;

38:23.646 --> 38:25.686 align:middle
wages are, you know,
if anything, a little,

38:25.686 --> 38:29.596 align:middle
still a bit above what would be
sustainable if productivity were

38:29.596 --> 38:31.526 align:middle
to revert to its
longer-run trend.

38:31.526 --> 38:34.746 align:middle
If you, if you take into account
the high productivity readings

38:34.746 --> 38:36.526 align:middle
we've had, then wages
are already

38:36.586 --> 38:38.956 align:middle
at a sustainable level
relative to 2 percent inflation.

38:39.626 --> 38:44.296 align:middle
So, again, I, I don't want
to overstate the downside

38:44.296 --> 38:48.046 align:middle
in the labor market, because
the downsides clearly appear

38:48.046 --> 38:49.116 align:middle
to have diminished.

38:49.606 --> 38:53.756 align:middle
Nonetheless, it's, it's one
of our mandate goals, and,

38:53.856 --> 38:56.006 align:middle
you know, we pay close
attention to it, and it's,

38:56.006 --> 38:58.206 align:middle
it's worth noting that
it is still gradually

38:58.206 --> 39:00.426 align:middle
cooling-gradually and
in an orderly way.

39:01.006 --> 39:02.836 align:middle
And, and, you know, that's,

39:02.906 --> 39:04.366 align:middle
that's how I would
characterize it,

39:04.366 --> 39:06.246 align:middle
and that's why we're
paying careful attention.

39:07.266 --> 39:07.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:07.756 --> 39:08.066 align:middle
Elizabeth.

39:10.256 --> 39:11.106 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

39:11.106 --> 39:11.866 align:middle
Thanks so much, Chair Powell.

39:11.866 --> 39:13.136 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze from ABC News.

39:13.136 --> 39:16.726 align:middle
As you've noted, the Fed is now
forecasting higher inflation

39:16.726 --> 39:17.206 align:middle
next year.

39:17.206 --> 39:19.436 align:middle
High prices are still a burden

39:19.436 --> 39:21.186 align:middle
for so many households
right now.

39:21.696 --> 39:24.106 align:middle
Why do you think it is
that inflation is proving

39:24.106 --> 39:26.476 align:middle
to be more stubborn
than you'd expected?

39:26.476 --> 39:29.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:30.806 --> 39:34.396 align:middle
You know, it, it breaks down
into a long answer, if you want,

39:34.436 --> 39:37.546 align:middle
but it just has been a
little bit more stubborn.

39:37.546 --> 39:39.516 align:middle
I think if you go back
two or three years,

39:40.036 --> 39:42.186 align:middle
many people were saying
that to get this far

39:42.186 --> 39:44.756 align:middle
down we would've had to have
a, had a deep recession and,

39:44.756 --> 39:46.476 align:middle
you know, high unemployment
by now.

39:46.476 --> 39:48.876 align:middle
Well, that has not been the
case, so, you know, the,

39:49.036 --> 39:53.106 align:middle
the path down has actually been
much better than many predicted.

39:53.416 --> 39:57.026 align:middle
We, we've managed to, to have
the unemployment rate remain

39:57.616 --> 40:00.976 align:middle
essentially at its
longer-run natural rate,

40:01.516 --> 40:04.746 align:middle
while inflation has
come down from-you know,

40:05.246 --> 40:08.456 align:middle
core PCE inflation has
come down from 5.6 percent

40:09.186 --> 40:11.456 align:middle
to 2.8 percent on
a 12-month basis.

40:11.456 --> 40:13.666 align:middle
So that's, that's a
pretty good outcome.

40:14.256 --> 40:15.356 align:middle
Why hasn't it come down [more]?

40:15.656 --> 40:19.556 align:middle
One reason is that,
that-just a technical issue

40:19.556 --> 40:23.666 align:middle
around the way we calculate
housing services, and that,

40:23.666 --> 40:27.436 align:middle
that process has been slower
than-market rents have,

40:27.436 --> 40:29.706 align:middle
are showing up more
slowly in that measure

40:30.176 --> 40:32.706 align:middle
than we might have thought
three, two, two years ago.

40:32.706 --> 40:33.616 align:middle
So that's part of it.

40:34.186 --> 40:36.956 align:middle
I think there are other,
other parts of the story, but,

40:37.136 --> 40:38.896 align:middle
you know, the, the-what,

40:38.896 --> 40:42.516 align:middle
what I think people are
feeling right now is the effect

40:42.516 --> 40:44.866 align:middle
of high prices, not
high inflation.

40:45.186 --> 40:48.216 align:middle
So we, we understand very
well that prices went

40:48.216 --> 40:51.186 align:middle
up by a great deal, and
people really feel that.

40:51.186 --> 40:53.846 align:middle
And it's prices of
food and transportation

40:53.846 --> 40:55.956 align:middle
and heating your home
and things like that.

40:55.956 --> 40:59.996 align:middle
So there's tremendous
pain in, in that burst

40:59.996 --> 41:01.636 align:middle
of inflation that
was very global.

41:01.636 --> 41:03.936 align:middle
This was everywhere in
all the advanced economies

41:03.936 --> 41:04.766 align:middle
at the same time.

41:05.366 --> 41:08.586 align:middle
So now we have-inflation
itself is way down,

41:08.586 --> 41:10.926 align:middle
but people are still
feeling high prices,

41:11.156 --> 41:13.736 align:middle
and that is-that is really
what people are feeling.

41:14.066 --> 41:17.796 align:middle
The best we can do for them-and
that's who we work for-is

41:17.796 --> 41:20.916 align:middle
to get inflation back down to
its target and keep it there

41:21.366 --> 41:25.786 align:middle
so that people are earning, you
know, big, real wage increases

41:25.786 --> 41:28.306 align:middle
so that their, their
wages are going up, their,

41:28.426 --> 41:30.326 align:middle
their compensation
is going up faster

41:30.326 --> 41:32.666 align:middle
than inflation year
upon year upon year.

41:33.026 --> 41:35.836 align:middle
And that's what will
restore people's good feeling

41:35.836 --> 41:36.586 align:middle
about the economy.

41:36.586 --> 41:38.276 align:middle
That's what it will take, and
that's what we're aiming for.

41:38.276 --> 41:38.666 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

41:38.666 --> 41:40.586 align:middle
And as we look ahead to
next year, what do you see

41:40.586 --> 41:42.506 align:middle
as the biggest challenge
to the economy

41:42.646 --> 41:43.696 align:middle
under the next Administration?

41:43.696 --> 41:43.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:43.916 --> 41:46.126 align:middle
I feel-I feel very good about
where the economy is, and,

41:46.126 --> 41:48.636 align:middle
honestly, I'm very optimistic
about, about the economy,

41:48.916 --> 41:52.776 align:middle
and it's-we're, we're in a
really good place; our policy's

41:52.776 --> 41:53.806 align:middle
in a really good place.

41:53.926 --> 41:56.546 align:middle
I, I expect another
good year next year.

41:57.186 --> 41:58.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:58.146 --> 42:00.986 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

42:01.566 --> 42:03.406 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell,
for doing the questions.

42:03.586 --> 42:04.916 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

42:05.236 --> 42:07.336 align:middle
So you say that we're
closer to the neutral rate.

42:07.336 --> 42:10.426 align:middle
What percent do you see, and
the Committee believes-where is

42:10.426 --> 42:10.986 align:middle
that neutral rate?

42:11.376 --> 42:11.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:12.466 --> 42:14.926 align:middle
So I'll say a couple things.

42:14.926 --> 42:17.866 align:middle
First of all, when we, when
we write-the thing we write

42:17.866 --> 42:20.586 align:middle
down in the Summary of Economic
Projections is the longer-run

42:20.776 --> 42:23.886 align:middle
neutral rate, which is
the neutral rate at a time

42:23.886 --> 42:26.626 align:middle
when supply and demand are in
balance, the full economy's

42:26.626 --> 42:28.536 align:middle
in balance, and no shocks
are hitting the economy.

42:28.976 --> 42:30.406 align:middle
That is not where
we are right now.

42:30.406 --> 42:34.106 align:middle
So when we're making
monetary policy at the Fed,

42:34.876 --> 42:36.156 align:middle
that's not the question
we're asking.

42:36.156 --> 42:39.746 align:middle
So you can't do a straight read
between those longer-run numbers

42:39.746 --> 42:42.146 align:middle
that we write down and what we
think the appropriate policy

42:42.146 --> 42:42.526 align:middle
should be.

42:43.246 --> 42:46.296 align:middle
So, basically, at
any given time,

42:46.676 --> 42:48.386 align:middle
various shocks are
hitting the economy.

42:48.386 --> 42:51.396 align:middle
And so what we're doing in,
in real time is we're looking

42:51.396 --> 42:53.526 align:middle
at our policy stance,
and we're looking

42:53.526 --> 42:55.896 align:middle
at the way it's hitting the
economy, and, particularly,

42:56.126 --> 42:57.886 align:middle
we look at the effects
it's having as we try

42:57.886 --> 43:00.336 align:middle
to move the economy
toward maximum employment

43:00.336 --> 43:01.116 align:middle
and price stability.

43:01.516 --> 43:05.146 align:middle
And the answer can be
that there are things

43:05.146 --> 43:06.156 align:middle
that affect the economy

43:06.156 --> 43:08.866 align:middle
that are-that are
lasting but not permanent.

43:09.186 --> 43:11.006 align:middle
And the, the ones that
are permanent are the ones

43:11.006 --> 43:12.186 align:middle
that would be in r*.

43:12.746 --> 43:14.676 align:middle
The ones that are,
could be lasting, but,

43:14.786 --> 43:16.296 align:middle
but nonetheless go
away over time,

43:16.746 --> 43:18.076 align:middle
they could actually affect what,

43:18.216 --> 43:21.556 align:middle
what's sort of technically
the appropriate neutral stance

43:21.886 --> 43:23.046 align:middle
in near term.

43:23.146 --> 43:25.296 align:middle
So we're looking at
that, and, you know,

43:25.296 --> 43:27.166 align:middle
we don't-we don't know
exactly where it is,

43:27.546 --> 43:30.876 align:middle
but as I like to say, we know it
by its works, and I think what,

43:30.876 --> 43:32.086 align:middle
what we know for sure is

43:32.086 --> 43:34.466 align:middle
that we're 100 basis points
closer to it right now.

43:35.046 --> 43:37.486 align:middle
There are many estimates
of where that might be,

43:37.486 --> 43:38.966 align:middle
and we know we're
a lot closer to it,

43:39.016 --> 43:40.746 align:middle
and I think we're
in a good place.

43:40.746 --> 43:44.026 align:middle
But I think from here it's a new
phase, and we're going to be,

43:44.146 --> 43:46.056 align:middle
you know, cautious
about, about further cuts.

43:46.056 --> 43:46.646 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

43:46.716 --> 43:48.566 align:middle
But, but I think that the
markets are looking for,

43:48.566 --> 43:49.516 align:middle
for a little more clarity.

43:49.516 --> 43:52.066 align:middle
I mean, I've heard estimates
from 2.9 percent to 4 percent.

43:52.496 --> 43:54.226 align:middle
You know, I, I think
the markets would

43:54.226 --> 43:56.376 align:middle
like to see a little more
clarity about a year out,

43:56.376 --> 43:58.886 align:middle
18 months out, as to
where that, that goalpost,

43:58.886 --> 43:59.806 align:middle
for lack of a better term, is,

43:59.806 --> 44:01.416 align:middle
because at the moment,
it looks big.

44:01.416 --> 44:02.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:02.346 --> 44:06.696 align:middle
Yeah, I mean, honestly, we-you
know, there are countless models

44:06.696 --> 44:09.166 align:middle
of what-of what a neutral rate
might be at any given time.

44:09.166 --> 44:10.466 align:middle
There, there are
empirical models,

44:10.466 --> 44:12.106 align:middle
there are theoretical
models, there are things

44:12.106 --> 44:13.706 align:middle
that combine them, and they have

44:13.986 --> 44:15.486 align:middle
as many different
answers as you'd like.

44:15.996 --> 44:17.896 align:middle
So there is no real certainty.

44:17.976 --> 44:20.456 align:middle
And, I mean, it's, it's
actually a good thing to know

44:21.096 --> 44:22.716 align:middle
that we don't know
exactly where it is,

44:22.986 --> 44:24.916 align:middle
so you're not-you're not
tempted to think, "Oh,

44:24.916 --> 44:26.886 align:middle
I think this model or
this estimate is right."

44:26.946 --> 44:30.846 align:middle
You, you just have to be open
to, you know, the empirical data

44:30.846 --> 44:33.076 align:middle
that are coming in and also
how it's affecting the outlook.

44:33.076 --> 44:36.006 align:middle
And it's not made any easier
by the fact that, you know,

44:36.006 --> 44:38.616 align:middle
that our policy works with
long and variable lags.

44:39.166 --> 44:42.216 align:middle
Nonetheless, that is the
job we have, and so we're,

44:42.356 --> 44:45.486 align:middle
we're- I think we need- it's
appropriate for us-for us now

44:45.486 --> 44:47.376 align:middle
to proceed cautiously, now

44:47.376 --> 44:49.326 align:middle
that we're 100 basis
points closer to,

44:49.816 --> 44:52.166 align:middle
to neutral, and we'll do so.

44:52.306 --> 44:54.836 align:middle
Meanwhile, the economy seems
to be in, in good shape,

44:55.406 --> 44:57.106 align:middle
and these cuts will
certainly help

44:57.106 --> 44:58.616 align:middle
to support economic activity

44:58.616 --> 45:01.696 align:middle
and the labor market while
we can still make progress

45:01.696 --> 45:04.176 align:middle
on inflation, because policy is
still meaningfully restrictive.

45:05.926 --> 45:06.726 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:07.386 --> 45:10.206 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

45:10.466 --> 45:11.736 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

45:11.736 --> 45:14.826 align:middle
I just wanted to make sure I
understood what you were saying

45:14.826 --> 45:15.916 align:middle
at the beginning
about inflation.

45:15.916 --> 45:20.126 align:middle
Are you saying that progress
on core PCE counts as progress

45:20.126 --> 45:24.526 align:middle
on inflation even if
headline inflation ticks up?

45:24.726 --> 45:28.116 align:middle
And then also, since that's
what's projected in the SEP,

45:28.116 --> 45:30.176 align:middle
I was wondering, what
accounts for that?

45:30.176 --> 45:35.096 align:middle
Why, why do you all see core
PCE going down and, potentially,

45:35.486 --> 45:37.466 align:middle
headline inflation ticking up?

45:37.846 --> 45:38.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:38.256 --> 45:42.086 align:middle
So, and as, as I imagine
you know, the, you know,

45:42.086 --> 45:44.386 align:middle
the goal overall is
headline inflation,

45:44.386 --> 45:45.776 align:middle
because that's what
people experience.

45:45.776 --> 45:47.596 align:middle
People don't experience
core inflation;

45:47.596 --> 45:48.846 align:middle
they experience inflation,

45:48.846 --> 45:50.936 align:middle
and that includes
food and energy costs.

45:50.936 --> 45:52.696 align:middle
So that's the overall goal.

45:53.106 --> 45:58.466 align:middle
But, as we know, headline
inflation contains energy

45:58.466 --> 46:01.016 align:middle
and food, and those prices
can fluctuate for reasons

46:01.016 --> 46:03.746 align:middle
that are not related to
tightness in the economy

46:03.746 --> 46:05.636 align:middle
and therefore are not
really good predictors

46:05.636 --> 46:06.586 align:middle
of future inflation.

46:06.896 --> 46:09.836 align:middle
So it turns out that
core inflation is,

46:09.876 --> 46:12.016 align:middle
is a better predictor
of overall inflation

46:12.516 --> 46:13.576 align:middle
than overall inflation is.

46:13.576 --> 46:16.526 align:middle
So we look at core inflation
because it's a better measure

46:16.526 --> 46:19.926 align:middle
of what future inflation is
like to be, likely to be,

46:19.926 --> 46:21.186 align:middle
because it's a better
measure of,

46:21.706 --> 46:23.626 align:middle
of what inflation
pressures exist.

46:23.826 --> 46:26.656 align:middle
So that's, that's-it's
complicated, but ultimately, we,

46:26.706 --> 46:29.046 align:middle
you know, our goal is
headline, not core.

46:29.406 --> 46:31.906 align:middle
So your question was, your
second question was what?

46:32.156 --> 46:32.516 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

46:32.516 --> 46:34.246 align:middle
Why do you all see
core going down

46:34.246 --> 46:35.646 align:middle
but headline potentially
ticking up?

46:36.536 --> 46:36.786 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:36.786 --> 46:39.096 align:middle
So headline can be
affected-you're talking

46:39.226 --> 46:40.266 align:middle
about next year.

46:40.396 --> 46:40.766 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

46:40.766 --> 46:41.296 align:middle
Mm-hmm. CHAIR POWELL.

46:41.626 --> 46:44.516 align:middle
Yeah, so headline can,
again, it can be affected

46:44.516 --> 46:46.116 align:middle
by energy prices
and food prices.

46:46.116 --> 46:48.926 align:middle
So that, there, there
will be things in,

46:48.926 --> 46:52.866 align:middle
in the headline forecasts
that are to do with forecasts

46:52.866 --> 46:55.666 align:middle
of energy prices,
whereas core will be-if,

46:55.666 --> 46:58.006 align:middle
if you look at core
going out a full year,

46:58.586 --> 47:01.116 align:middle
then it'll be much
more driven by things

47:01.116 --> 47:03.746 align:middle
like tightness in the economy.

47:03.876 --> 47:07.786 align:middle
So it-that, that's why the two
can go in different directions.

47:07.786 --> 47:10.976 align:middle
Headline has been lower, as
you know, most of this year,

47:10.976 --> 47:13.286 align:middle
but that's because energy
prices have been coming down,

47:13.656 --> 47:14.946 align:middle
which is a great
thing for people.

47:14.946 --> 47:18.006 align:middle
But energy prices will come
down, and then they will go up,

47:18.006 --> 47:19.636 align:middle
and it won't really
be telling us anything

47:19.636 --> 47:21.666 align:middle
about how tight the
economy is and how,

47:21.976 --> 47:23.156 align:middle
how future inflation
will perform.

47:23.156 --> 47:23.976 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

47:23.976 --> 47:25.686 align:middle
Do geopolitical risks
factor in at all

47:25.756 --> 47:27.846 align:middle
to how you're thinking
about energy prices?

47:27.846 --> 47:28.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:28.596 --> 47:31.756 align:middle
So we, we monitor geopolitical
risks really quickly, but,

47:31.846 --> 47:33.046 align:middle
you know, but-really
carefully, rather.

47:33.196 --> 47:35.546 align:middle
But, you know, I would say

47:36.296 --> 47:39.186 align:middle
so far those risks haven't
really-nothing has come

47:39.186 --> 47:41.366 align:middle
out of those risks that really,
has really been important

47:41.366 --> 47:42.496 align:middle
for the United States economy.

47:42.496 --> 47:45.706 align:middle
The single thing that you would
look to is the price of oil,

47:45.786 --> 47:48.566 align:middle
given that we're talking about
the Middle East and Ukraine,

47:48.566 --> 47:52.626 align:middle
and, you know, and-but that,
that is a good summary statistic

47:52.626 --> 47:55.316 align:middle
for the kind of thing
that could go wrong in,

47:55.506 --> 47:56.746 align:middle
in, with global turmoil.

47:56.746 --> 47:59.466 align:middle
But the price of oil's
been coming down, and, for,

47:59.616 --> 48:02.636 align:middle
because of supply conditions,
global supply conditions.

48:02.636 --> 48:06.016 align:middle
So we don't-the U.S.
is not feeling, really,

48:06.016 --> 48:09.036 align:middle
the effects of geopolitical
turmoil, but we are at,

48:09.036 --> 48:12.096 align:middle
certainly, at a time of
elevated geopolitical turmoil.

48:12.096 --> 48:13.886 align:middle
And it remains, you
know, a risk.

48:15.136 --> 48:16.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:16.346 --> 48:18.576 align:middle
Kelly. KELLY O'GRADY.

48:18.806 --> 48:19.516 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

48:19.516 --> 48:20.506 align:middle
Kelly O'Grady from CBS News.

48:20.506 --> 48:23.396 align:middle
I want to go back to something
that you said a minute ago,

48:23.396 --> 48:26.476 align:middle
that wage growth is
outpacing inflation now.

48:26.606 --> 48:28.766 align:middle
It wasn't the case for
some time, of course.

48:28.766 --> 48:32.386 align:middle
It's partly why Americans
haven't felt much relief

48:32.386 --> 48:33.736 align:middle
in their wallets
from prices yet.

48:34.206 --> 48:37.846 align:middle
But with inflation ticking up,
how worried are you the progress

48:37.946 --> 48:39.656 align:middle
in closing that gap
could go away?

48:39.656 --> 48:40.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:40.816 --> 48:45.056 align:middle
Yeah, so inflation-again, we
don't overreact to a couple

48:45.056 --> 48:46.086 align:middle
of months of higher readings

48:46.086 --> 48:47.426 align:middle
or a couple months
of lower readings.

48:47.426 --> 48:49.366 align:middle
And we, what we had
was-we had four months

48:49.366 --> 48:51.506 align:middle
of really nice readings,
and then September

48:51.506 --> 48:54.616 align:middle
and October were higher, but
then November is much lower.

48:54.616 --> 48:57.816 align:middle
So I don't really think
the public is experiencing

48:57.816 --> 49:01.706 align:middle
that as a surprising
upside risk to inflation.

49:01.706 --> 49:03.566 align:middle
I, I think inflation
is much lower.

49:03.896 --> 49:06.356 align:middle
What the public is feeling,
and they're right about it,

49:06.436 --> 49:09.676 align:middle
is that prices are, are
just-the price level went up,

49:09.676 --> 49:11.466 align:middle
because of the past inflation.

49:11.946 --> 49:15.876 align:middle
It is going to take some time
to, for real wages to recover,

49:15.876 --> 49:18.366 align:middle
over a period of
years in which your,

49:18.436 --> 49:20.586 align:middle
your real compensation
is growing.

49:21.186 --> 49:21.946 align:middle
And, in other words,

49:21.946 --> 49:23.936 align:middle
your compensation's
growing meaningfully faster

49:23.936 --> 49:24.616 align:middle
than inflation.

49:24.946 --> 49:27.276 align:middle
That's exactly the kind
of economy we have now,

49:27.796 --> 49:29.416 align:middle
and we just want
to hold onto it.

49:29.416 --> 49:32.516 align:middle
That, that process will probably
take some years, but that's,

49:32.606 --> 49:33.836 align:middle
that's what's going
on right now.

49:34.046 --> 49:37.506 align:middle
And I don't think that, you
know, a, a couple of months

49:37.506 --> 49:40.086 align:middle
of higher inflation
really signal that,

49:40.306 --> 49:42.626 align:middle
anything of the nature
you're suggesting.

49:42.716 --> 49:43.176 align:middle
KELLY O'GRADY.

49:43.306 --> 49:45.746 align:middle
And just one follow-up:
Let's look more long-term.

49:45.746 --> 49:49.346 align:middle
You previously predicted hitting
the 2 percent inflation target

49:49.346 --> 49:50.866 align:middle
in 2026.

49:50.866 --> 49:53.486 align:middle
It's now been pushed
out to 2027.

49:53.636 --> 49:54.776 align:middle
You said you're focused

49:54.776 --> 49:57.836 align:middle
on enabling further
progress on inflation.

49:57.986 --> 50:00.526 align:middle
That's not necessarily
progress in the right direction.

50:00.886 --> 50:04.036 align:middle
Are you confident that target
isn't going to move further out?

50:04.036 --> 50:04.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:05.036 --> 50:07.496 align:middle
We're talking about-when
you're projecting the economy,

50:08.086 --> 50:10.776 align:middle
you know, three years out,
two years out, you're talking

50:10.776 --> 50:11.816 align:middle
about high uncertainty.

50:12.346 --> 50:13.516 align:middle
Very high uncertainty.

50:13.956 --> 50:17.956 align:middle
You know, we, we really-at
that point it, it's not,

50:18.576 --> 50:20.796 align:middle
it's not possible to
confidently predict

50:20.796 --> 50:22.536 align:middle
where the economy is going
to be in three years.

50:22.916 --> 50:25.246 align:middle
So what we're doing is we're
looking at what's happening now.

50:25.246 --> 50:27.766 align:middle
And we're kind of
projecting that the same kinds

50:27.766 --> 50:31.346 align:middle
of things are happening-so we,
we keep a strong labor market,

50:31.826 --> 50:34.616 align:middle
housing services
inflation comes down,

50:34.616 --> 50:38.026 align:middle
goods and services-goods
inflation settles down, and,

50:38.326 --> 50:41.186 align:middle
you know, nonmarket
services return

50:41.186 --> 50:42.776 align:middle
to their, their prior level.

50:43.296 --> 50:45.906 align:middle
All of those things should
happen over time, and, and,

50:45.906 --> 50:47.346 align:middle
you know, those pieces
come together.

50:47.346 --> 50:48.826 align:middle
There's every reason to
think that they will.

50:49.136 --> 50:50.696 align:middle
The timing of it is
highly uncertain.

50:50.926 --> 50:53.926 align:middle
You know, but you're not wrong,
though, that, you know, we've,

50:54.176 --> 50:56.076 align:middle
the- it's been a bit frustrating

50:56.076 --> 50:58.436 align:middle
because the-while
we've made progress,

50:58.556 --> 51:00.176 align:middle
it has been slower
than we had hoped.

51:00.796 --> 51:02.166 align:middle
Nonetheless, we're
still on track.

51:02.166 --> 51:04.476 align:middle
And, you know, I think if
you'd-if two, two years ago,

51:04.476 --> 51:07.606 align:middle
you'd said, "we were [going to
be] at 4.2 percent unemployment

51:07.606 --> 51:10.226 align:middle
and 2.8 percent inflation,"
people would be,

51:10.266 --> 51:11.226 align:middle
would say, "I'll take that."

51:11.226 --> 51:15.316 align:middle
I mean, that, that's a pretty
good interim place to be.

51:15.926 --> 51:19.706 align:middle
Job's not done, but, but I
think we're, we're feeling good

51:19.706 --> 51:21.096 align:middle
about where we are and
where we're headed.

51:22.206 --> 51:22.506 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:22.506 --> 51:23.646 align:middle
Go to Nancy for the
last question.

51:27.576 --> 51:28.126 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

51:28.126 --> 51:28.636 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

51:28.636 --> 51:30.716 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

51:31.106 --> 51:32.596 align:middle
You said a couple times,

51:32.596 --> 51:34.906 align:middle
inflation has been
moving sideways.

51:35.326 --> 51:37.596 align:middle
You know, it appears to
be settling in around,

51:37.886 --> 51:39.956 align:middle
excuse me, 2½ percent.

51:40.656 --> 51:44.046 align:middle
Do you think the Fed is just
going to have to settle for that

51:44.046 --> 51:47.816 align:middle
and accept that you're not going
to get to your 2 percent target?

51:47.816 --> 51:49.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:49.236 --> 51:50.746 align:middle
No, we're not going
to settle for that.

51:50.746 --> 51:52.086 align:middle
I think we're, we're, you know,

51:52.086 --> 51:55.706 align:middle
we certainly have every
intention and expectations

51:55.706 --> 51:58.486 align:middle
that we'll get inflation back
sustainably to 2 percent.

51:59.116 --> 52:01.696 align:middle
That is-and I am confident
we will achieve that.

52:02.266 --> 52:06.206 align:middle
It has taken longer, but
it's, you have to be-you know,

52:06.206 --> 52:07.446 align:middle
we are making progress.

52:07.446 --> 52:09.296 align:middle
We have made a great
deal of progress.

52:09.296 --> 52:12.576 align:middle
And we'll continue to do so and
get back to 2 percent inflation.

52:12.576 --> 52:13.756 align:middle
That's what we owe the public.

52:13.756 --> 52:15.476 align:middle
And, and, you know, we're
committed to achieving it.

52:15.706 --> 52:16.326 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

52:16.326 --> 52:19.566 align:middle
In that case, can you rule
out a rate hike next year?

52:19.566 --> 52:20.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:21.636 --> 52:25.926 align:middle
You don't rule things completely
in or out in this-in this world.

52:26.026 --> 52:29.216 align:middle
That doesn't appear to
be a likely outcome.

52:29.436 --> 52:31.266 align:middle
I think we're at 4.3 percent.

52:31.396 --> 52:34.836 align:middle
That's, that's meaningfully
restrictive, and I,

52:35.486 --> 52:39.186 align:middle
I think it's a well-calibrated
rate for us to continue

52:39.186 --> 52:41.596 align:middle
to make progress on inflation
while, while keeping a,

52:41.596 --> 52:42.526 align:middle
a strong labor market.

52:43.076 --> 52:54.516 align:middle
So-Thank you very much.

